  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 408 PM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nThis is where ensemble members start to struggle with agreeing on \nthe evolution of the upper-level pattern. We'll focus mostly on how \nit relates to the south Florida region for this discussion. A third \nshortwave is expected to dive out of the Great Lakes region and \nNortheast through Saturday, while the aforementioned second \nshortwave meanders across southern FL and the SW Atlantic. Global \nensembles are uncertain as to whether this southernmost shortwave \nwill cutoff from the mean northern stream trough, or remain an open \nwave that stays connected with the latter shortwave and northern \nstream. The implications of this seem to be (1) how low of a \nlatitude does this upper low reach (2) is there a pathway for any \nresultant surface feature to draw nearer to the east coast of FL. \nThe further south the trough reaches, the further south the heavy \nrain potential may reach (considering also the next point). If the \ntrough cuts off fully (or even somewhat) it may open a pathway for \nany surface reflection that develops to get pulled back westward \nbefore eventually getting pushed into the Atlantic as the longwave \ntrough progresses east. \n\nAs far as the anticipated rain amounts are concerned, the best \nutility we have at this moment to assess potential impacts is the \nWPC QPF clustering tool. For several days guidance has been \nclustered into a higher QPF and lower QPF scenario that's likely \nrelated to how far west any surface feature may be able to reach. \nFurther, the higher QPF solution has a north/south scenario that's \nlikely tied to how far south the southern extent of the longwave \ntrough reaches. Unfortunately ensemble members are pretty evenly \nsplit between the higher/lower QPF scenario, and also split within \nthe higher QPF scenario as to where the heaviest rain may fall. \nHere's what we can say:\n* Over the 48 hour period from Sunday through Monday the east coast \n  metros are expected to be wet, with at least widespread 1-2\" of \n  rain. This could also mean localized higher amounts nearly double \n  those values.\n* There's a medium (50%) chance that the widespread average amounts \n  could be closer to 3-5\", with an isolated location or two \n  potentially receiving 7-9\".\n* If the heavy rain scenario pans out it's too early to tell exactly \n  where these amount may fall. It could be anywhere from Cape \n  Canaveral to Homestead.\n\nAnother threat to consider is the potential for strong and gusty \nwinds Sunday and Monday. There's a medium chance (40-60%) that along \nthe east coast metros sustained winds could reach 25 mph with gusts \naround 35 mph. This could result in scattered downed limbs and \nfronds and displacement of unsecured light-weight items.\n\nWhile the potential for heavy rain and strong winds may subside a \nbit past Monday, we're still unlikely to dry out completely through \nnext week as we remain under the influence of solid easterly flow. \nThis would result period of scattered showers moving inland from the \nAtlantic waters each day.",
